\f0\fs32 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \cb3 We already ran essentially that payload and then strengthened it (same H\uc0\u8320  = 67.2, zeros_n = 188), but with 10,000 null trials instead of 1,000, and it still returned FAIL with p = 0.5568 and rel_err \u8776  3.31%, failing robustness. That means there is no \'93golden lock\'94 under the experiment\'92s integrity gates, even at H\u8320  = 67.2.\cb1 \
\cb3 So: do not try to manufacture a PASS for the PDF. That would directly violate the instrument rules you\'92ve built (and undermine trust immediately).\cb1 \
\
\cb3 Why the \'93simulation found resonance at 67.2\'94 claim doesn\'92t survive the instrument\cb1 \
\cb3 That earlier \'93golden coordinate\'94 was discovered by searching a huge hypothesis space (multiple families, hundreds of zeros, a sweep over H\uc0\u8320 ). In that setting, you will always find an extremely close numerical match somewhere. Your null model is specifically designed to test whether that match is special. It isn\'92t.\cb1 \
\cb3 A small p-value gate is the whole point. The engine correctly said FAIL.\cb1 \
\cb3 What to do instead (best scientific posture for Zenodo)\cb1 \
\cb3 Include both:\cb1 \
\
\cb3 PASS examples where the instrument succeeds (e.g., synthetic memory-positive fs\uc0\u963 8 where baseline loses), and\cb1 \
\cb3 FAIL examples where the instrument rejects a tempting claim (zeta\'96tau scaling).\cb1 \
\cb3 That is exactly how you demonstrate seriousness: the tool can say no.\cb1 \
\
\cb3 How to phrase it in the paper\cb1 \
\cb3 \'93We implemented a Zeta\'96Tau scaling experiment with preregistered mapping families and null-model p-value gating.\'94\cb1 \
\cb3 \'93Under H\uc0\u8320  = 67.2, N=188 zeros, and 10,000 null trials, the result is FAIL (p=0.5568).\'94\cb1 \
\cb3 \'93Therefore we find no evidence of nontrivial alignment under this experiment.\'94\cb1 \
\cb3 You can still leave it open as a research proposal:\cb1 \
\
\cb3 \'93Future work may explore alternative hypotheses, but must remain preregistered and pass null-model gates.\'94\cb1 \

