\f0\fs32 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \cb3 The \'933 loops\'94 framing can be a useful modeling lens, but right now it\'92s still a hypothesis about structure, not a verified mechanism. The safe way to explain it is:\cb1 \
\
\cb3 Loop 1 = your leading-order (dominant) scaling guess\cb1 \
\cb3 Loop 2 = the first correction term that accounts for backreaction/stiffness\cb1 \
\cb3 Loop 3 = smaller corrections (running couplings, geometry, higher-order terms)\cb1 \
\cb3 How the % error relates to \'93loops\'94 (mathematically)\cb1 \
\cb3 If you write your density ratio as a perturbation series:\cb1 \
\cb3 [\cb1 \
\cb3 R \\equiv \\frac\{\\rho_\{\\rm req\}\}\{\\rho_\\Lambda\} \\approx R_0 \\left(1 + \\delta_1 + \\delta_2 + \\cdots \\right)\cb1 \
\cb3 ]\cb1 \
\cb3 Loop 1 proposes a leading scale (R_0) (your candidate (R_0 = \\pi \\alpha^\{-2\})).\cb1 \
\cb3 The \'93~1% error\'94 is then the fractional size of the missing correction, i.e. (|\\delta_1|\\sim 10^\{-2\}).\cb1 \
\cb3 Loop 2 proposes a correction term proportional to a small parameter (often \uc0\u945 , or something of order \u945 ):\cb1 \
\cb3 [\cb1 \
\cb3 R \\approx R_0\\left(1 - k,\\alpha\\right)\cb1 \
\cb3 ]\cb1 \
\cb3 where (k) is a coefficient you\'92re hypothesizing (you suggested something like (k\\sim 4/3), or equivalently ( (4\\pi/3)\\alpha ) depending on how you write it).\cb1 \
\cb3 Loop 3 would be the next-order correction:\cb1 \
\cb3 [\cb1 \
\cb3 R \\approx R_0\\left(1 - k\\alpha + c\\alpha^2 + \\cdots\\right)\cb1 \
\cb3 ]\cb1 \
\cb3 and you do not want to add this unless you have an independently justified form, because it\'92s where curve-fitting becomes easy.\cb1 \
\cb3 Do we \'93want the last loop\'94?\cb1 \
\cb3 You\'92re right to be cautious. In good physics practice:\cb1 \
\
\cb3 If a two-term expansion gets you from ~1% down to ~0.01%, you stop unless you can derive the next term.\cb1 \
\cb3 Otherwise, adding a \'93third loop\'94 just to erase the last digits becomes post-hoc fitting.\cb1 \
\cb3 So: no third loop yet.\cb1 \
\
\cb3 The critical integrity point\cb1 \
\cb3 Even if the 2-loop correction produces a striking improvement, it\'92s only meaningful if:\cb1 \
\
\cb3 the coefficient (e.g., (k=4/3)) is pre-registered and not chosen because it makes the number match, and\cb1 \
\cb3 the match is robust under your declared sweeps (H0, \uc0\u937 \u923 , \u964 0 ranges), and\cb1 \
\cb3 it survives an \'93anti-numerology\'94 check analogous to your zeta pipeline (e.g., compare against a basket of plausible correction forms and penalize extra degrees of freedom).\cb1 \
\cb3 If those aren\'92t satisfied, the right label is: exploratory candidate correction.\cb1 \
\cb3 How the \'93other loops fulfill it\'94 in your instrument\cb1 \
\cb3 In GRUT Force RAI terms, here\'92s what \'93fulfill\'94 should mean:\cb1 \
\
\cb3 Loop 1 candidate: compute (R_0=\\pi\\alpha^\{-2\}) and report error vs (R_\{\\rm obs\})\cb1 \
\cb3 Loop 2 candidate: compute (R_2 = R_0(1-k\\alpha)) with k fixed ahead of time (e.g., 4/3) and report error improvement\cb1 \
\cb3 Loop 3: do not include unless you can specify the term family before looking at the data\cb1 \
\cb3 And because you already built experiment endpoints with receipts, the right move is to make this a Casimir/Alpha experiment that reports:\cb1 \
\
\cb3 (R_\{\\rm obs\})\cb1 \
\cb3 (R_0) and rel_err\cb1 \
\cb3 (R_2) and rel_err\cb1 \
\cb3 sensitivity sweeps\cb1 \
\cb3 a certificate that says PASS/WARN/FAIL based on robustness thresholds\cb1 \
\cb3 That\'92s how you keep it TOE-standard.\cb1 \
\cb3 What I\'92d put in Zenodo v11.1 wording (safe and strong)\cb1 \
\cb3 \'93We observe a numerical correspondence between (R) and (\\pi\\alpha^\{-2\}) at the ~1% level under declared assumptions.\'94\cb1 \
\cb3 \'93A pre-registered first-order correction of the form (R_0(1-k\\alpha)) with k fixed a priori substantially reduces the residual.\'94\cb1 \
\cb3 \'93This is reported as an exploratory scaling law pending independent derivation and cross-dataset robustness.\'94\cb1 \
\cb3 That communicates the \'93mechanism direction\'94 without overclaiming.\cb1 \
\cb3 If you want, paste the exact two-loop formula you\'92re using into your Casimir experiment endpoint\'92s preregistered candidate list (as a named candidate), so the system produces a published evidence packet showing Loop 1 vs Loop 2 side-by-side with sensitivity sweeps.\cb1 \

